# Spinner

An indeterminate loading indicator for an inline wait with no known
shape or duration. Basecoat ships no component — it is the
`loader-circle` lucide icon with `animate-spin`, `role="status"` and an
`aria-label`, written inline. Size with `size-*`.

<%= embed :spinner, :default %>

```html
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
     stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"
     stroke-linejoin="round"
     role="status" aria-label="Loading" class="animate-spin size-6">
  <path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-6.219-8.56" />
</svg>
```

## When to use

- An inline region is fetching and its eventual layout is unknown.
- A small, short wait where a skeleton would be heavier than the
  content it stands in for.

## When not to use

- A loading page region whose layout is known — use `skeleton`
  shaped like the content (see the *Feedback* pattern page).
- An in-flight button action — use the button's own `loading` param,
  not a spinner next to the button.
- Measurable progress — use `progress`.

## Anatomy and options

The `loader-circle` SVG with `animate-spin` for the rotation and a
`size-*` for the dimensions (`size-4` inline, `size-6`/`size-8`
standalone). Colour follows `currentColor`, so it inherits the text
colour of its context.

## Do and don't

- Do set a specific `aria-label` when the context isn't obvious:
  `aria-label="Searching patients"`.
- Do remove the spinner the moment content (or its empty state)
  arrives — never alongside it.
- Don't show more than one loading signal for the same wait (see
  *Feedback*: one signal at a time).
- Don't use a spinner for waits the user didn't initiate — content
  that loads with the page just appears.

## Accessibility and content

- `role="status"` with `aria-label` lets assistive tech announce the
  wait without any extra markup.
- The label names what is loading, not the mechanism: "Searching
  patients", not "Please wait".
